 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers remaining tabs contiguously
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: after a tab is closed, remaining tabs are assigned `t1` through `tN` in their existing order. `next` becomes `N + 1`, so the next opened tab extends the contiguous sequence.
+- Override: the maintainer explicitly overrode the 2026-09-02 decision that keys never shift after close.
+- Tradeoff: because each key is both the React key and URL state, closing a tab can remount tabs to its right and drop their scroll position or unsaved form input. Contiguous keys are now the requested behavior.
 - Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
